      "sequence_position": 10,
      "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}+1?$",
      "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275437/assets/664a9c365070244d6c1e0047b140d9d108d9a03cf4fe32eec4694236e8740e8f.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,3)$ to $(0,-3)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275437/assets/00256f0296c36170d317afc06316d7920f9b2a1f89dfa534eca91cb5e395b6ee.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $1$ unit up to give $y=-3{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}+1.$ The point $(0,-3)$ moves to $(0,-2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275437/assets/662ad8370cf4c696bf831544bf425a28223a74a63cbf959521f69a7166b4dfc8.png)",
